</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-11-pelham-parkway/">← Back to District 11 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 11 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 11. Remember, effective communication is key to ensuring your child receives the appropriate special education services. This directory provides contact information and critical advice to navigate the special education system in District 11.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 2 (Serving District 11)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>3450 East Tremont Ave, Bronx, NY 10465</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-794-7420">718-794-7420</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Note:</strong> Using your NYC Schools Account (NYCSA) for communication provides a documented record. If you don't have an account, create one. All official communication should be traceable.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt (date, time, method of contact) and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep a detailed log of all communication attempts. This log will be crucial if you need to file a complaint later.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This applies to initial referrals, requests for independent educational evaluations (IEEs), and any other formal requests.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. Example: "Per our conversation on [date] at [time], this email confirms my request for [specific action, e.g., an evaluation for speech therapy]."</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> (or "Request for IEE - [Student Name]", etc.)</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of contact (phone, email), and a brief summary of the communication. Store all emails in a dedicated folder.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response within a reasonable timeframe, follow this escalation ladder. "Reasonable timeframe" generally means 2-3 business days for an acknowledgement, and longer for a substantive response depending on the complexity of the issue.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Ensure you have a record of the contact.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal, if applicable to the issue). State clearly that this is a second attempt to reach them.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Politely state that you have not received a response from the CSE and are seeking their assistance in resolving the issue. Include all previous emails in the chain.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> If the issue remains unresolved and involves a denial of Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE), file a "State Complaint" with NYSED. Instructions can be found on the NYSED website. You can also consider requesting mediation or filing for an impartial hearing (due process).</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa- Bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 11
    </h2>

    <div class="advocacy-tips" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), New York State Part 200 Regulations, and the NYC Department of Education's policies on special education. Knowledge is power.</li>
            <li><strong>Document Everything:</strong> Maintain a detailed record of all communication, meetings, evaluations, and IEPs. This documentation is essential if you need to advocate for your child's needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Attend CSE Meetings Prepared:</strong> Review your child's IEP, evaluations, and progress reports before each meeting. Prepare a list of questions and concerns to discuss. Bring an advocate or support person if you feel overwhelmed.</li>
            <li><strong>Request Evaluations:</strong> If you believe your child needs an evaluation in a specific area (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, psychological evaluation), make a formal written request to the CSE. The district must respond to your request within a reasonable timeframe.</li>
            <li><strong>Understand the IEP Process:</strong> The IEP is a legally binding document that outlines your child's special education needs and services. Ensure that the IEP is individualized to meet your child's specific needs and includes measurable goals. Advocate for accommodations and modifications that will support your child's success.</li>
            <li><strong>Consider an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</strong> If you disagree with the district's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense, under certain conditions (8 NYCRR §200.5(i)). This can provide a valuable second opinion.</li>
	    <li><strong>Participate Actively:</strong> Your input as a parent is invaluable. Actively participate in all stages of the special education process, from initial referral to IEP development and implementation.</li>
            <li><strong>Network with Other Parents:</strong> Connect with other parents of children with special needs in District 11. Sharing experiences and resources can provide valuable support and guidance.</li>
	    <li><strong>Be Persistent and Patient:</strong> Navigating the special education system can be challenging. Be persistent in advocating for your child's needs and patient throughout the process.</li>

        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-Exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 11 Pitfalls (and How to Avoid Them)
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfalls-list" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Delay in Evaluations:</strong> Districts sometimes delay evaluations. Know that NYS regulations state a timeline. If delays occur, send a certified letter reiterating your request and citing 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii) regarding the 60-day timeline.</li>
            <li><strong>Inadequate IEP Goals:</strong> IEP goals should be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART). If goals are vague or not aligned with your child's needs, request a revision of the IEP. Provide specific examples of what you want the goal to address.</li>
            <li><strong>Lack of Related Services:</strong> Ensure that your child receives all necessary related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling) as specified in the IEP. If services are not being provided consistently, document the missed sessions and request compensatory services.</li>
            <li><strong>Placement Issues:</strong> The IEP team must consider the least restrictive environment (LRE) for your child. If you believe your child's current placement is not appropriate, request a meeting to discuss alternative placements. Be prepared to suggest specific placement options that you believe would be a better fit.</li>
            <li><strong>Failure to Implement IEP:</strong> The district is legally obligated to implement the IEP as written. If the IEP is not being followed, document the specific instances of non-compliance and request a meeting with the CSE to address the issue. If the issue persists, consider filing a State Complaint.</li>
	    <li><strong>Predetermined Outcomes:</strong> Ensure the CSE is not coming to meetings with predetermined outcomes. The IEP must be individualized to your child. If you believe the CSE is not considering your input, document your concerns and request a meeting with a higher-level administrator.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-Question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-items" style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">What is the first step if I suspect my child has a disability?</h3>
            <p>The first step is to submit a written referral to the CSE. Include your concerns and any supporting documentation (e.g., doctor's reports, school records). The district is then required to evaluate your child within 60 school days of receiving your written consent for the evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">What if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</h3>
            <p>You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation. You must request the IEE in writing. The district can either agree to pay for the IEE or initiate an impartial hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate (8 NYCRR §200.5(i)).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">What is an IEP and what should it include?</h3>
            <p>An IEP (Individualized Education Program) is a written plan that outlines the special education services and supports that a child with a disability needs to succeed in school. It should include present levels of performance, measurable annual goals, a statement of services and supports, a statement of accommodations and modifications, and a statement of how the child will participate in state and district-wide assessments. Ensure all components are specific and tailored to your child’s unique needs, as required under IDEA.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">What can I do if the school is not following my child's IEP?</h3>
            <p>Document all instances where the IEP is not being followed. Communicate your concerns in writing to the CSE and request a meeting to address the issue. If the issue is not resolved, you can file a State Complaint with NYSED or request mediation or an impartial hearing.</p>
        </div>

	<div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York?</h3>
            <p>You can find more information on the New York State Education Department (NYSED) website, the NYC Department of Education website, and through various parent advocacy organizations. Consider contacting your local Parent Training and Information Center (PTI) for support and resources.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
    </div>
</main>
